1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,286
Hi! Travel stories are always exciting.

2
00:00:03,286 --> 00:00:09,353
Today we'll learn to talk about places you've visited with '가 본 적이 있어요',

3
00:00:09,353 --> 00:00:12,050
and future plans with '할 계획이에요' [hal-gye-hoek-i-e-yo].

4
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:21,849
Just two things: talking about your past '경험' [gyeong-heom] (experience),

5
00:00:21,849 --> 00:00:24,464
and your future '계획' [gye-hoek] (plans).

6
00:00:24,464 --> 00:00:28,070
Let's connect past and future naturally.

7
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,119
Some places out there you've been to,

8
00:00:35,119 --> 00:00:36,813
others you want to visit, right?

9
00:00:36,813 --> 00:00:38,560
Let's say that feeling in Korean.

10
00:00:42,470 --> 00:00:47,849
'여행' [yeo-haeng] means travel — going somewhere and coming back.

11
00:00:47,849 --> 00:00:51,263
Use it like '여행을 가다' [yeo-haeng-eul-ga-da] or '여행을 하다' [yeo-haeng-eul-ha-da].

12
00:00:51,263 --> 00:00:54,780
In summer I travel with my family.

13
00:00:55,930 --> 00:01:00,756
'경험' [gyeong-heom] means experience — something you've done.

14
00:01:00,756 --> 00:01:03,589
Travel '경험' [gyeong-heom] stays with you.

15
00:01:03,589 --> 00:01:08,100
Say '좋은 경험이었어요' [jot-eun-gyeong-heom-i-eot-eo-yo] — it was a good experience.

16
00:01:11,000 --> 00:01:15,552
'명소' [myeong-so] means a famous spot many people visit.

17
00:01:15,552 --> 00:01:18,305
Every city has great '명소' [myeong-so].

18
00:01:18,305 --> 00:01:22,540
That one out there is a well-known '명소' [myeong-so].

19
00:01:27,990 --> 00:01:32,922
'예약' [ye-yak] means reservation — booking in advance.

20
00:01:32,922 --> 00:01:37,629
Before a trip, you '예약' [ye-yak] tickets and rooms.

21
00:01:37,629 --> 00:01:40,880
Ask '예약했어요? ' [ye-yak-haet-eo-yo-?] — did you book?

22
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:48,939
'숙소' [suk-so] means accommodation — where you sleep on a trip.

23
00:01:48,939 --> 00:01:52,429
Hotels and guesthouses are all '숙소' [suk-so].

24
00:01:52,429 --> 00:01:56,210
'숙소를 예약했어요' [suk-so-reul-ye-yak-haet-eo-yo] — I booked a place to stay.

25
00:01:57,950 --> 00:02:02,074
'계획' [gye-hoek] means plan — what you've decided to do.

26
00:02:02,074 --> 00:02:04,887
Use '~할 계획이에요' [~-hal-gye-hoek-i-e-yo] for the future.

27
00:02:04,887 --> 00:02:07,980
I plan to go hiking this weekend.

28
00:02:10,950 --> 00:02:12,662
Now, experiences.

29
00:02:12,662 --> 00:02:18,501
Add '에 가 본 적이 있어요' after a place to say you've been there.

30
00:02:18,501 --> 00:02:21,320
Repeat slowly: '가 본 적이 있어요'.

31
00:02:27,770 --> 00:02:32,304
For example: '제주도에 가 본 적이 있어요' — I've been to Jeju Island,

32
00:02:32,304 --> 00:02:34,883
a place with beautiful blue seas.

33
00:02:34,883 --> 00:02:37,150
Point far away as you say it.

34
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:42,593
One more: '서울에 가 본 적이 있어요' — I've been to Seoul.

35
00:02:42,593 --> 00:02:46,290
Where have you been? Think of it and say it.

36
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:54,565
Add how many times: '두 번 가 본 적이 있어요' — I've been twice.

37
00:02:54,565 --> 00:02:58,210
If many times: '여러 번 가 본 적이 있어요'.

38
00:02:59,790 --> 00:03:07,123
Use it for any experience: '한 적이 있어요' [han-jeok-i-it-eo-yo] means you have the experience; if not,

39
00:03:07,123 --> 00:03:08,860
answer '한 적이 없어요' [han-jeok-i-eop-eo-yo].

40
00:03:12,650 --> 00:03:14,504
Now the future.

41
00:03:14,504 --> 00:03:22,663
Add '할 계획이에요' [hal-gye-hoek-i-e-yo] after what you intend to do: '갈 계획이에요' [gal-gye-hoek-i-e-yo] (plan to go),

42
00:03:22,663 --> 00:03:25,630
'볼 계획이에요' [bol-gye-hoek-i-e-yo] (plan to see).

43
00:03:27,070 --> 00:03:30,075
For example: '다음엔 부산에 갈 계획이에요' — Next,

44
00:03:30,075 --> 00:03:31,815
I plan to go to Busan,

45
00:03:31,815 --> 00:03:34,584
famous for its sea and night views.

46
00:03:34,584 --> 00:03:37,510
Say it dreamily, watching the sunset.

47
00:03:37,960 --> 00:03:45,690
Not just places — also things to do: '주말에 등산할 계획이에요' [ju-mal-e-deung-san-hal-gye-hoek-i-e-yo] — I plan to hike this weekend.

48
00:03:45,690 --> 00:03:48,950
'쉴 계획이에요' [swil-gye-hoek-i-e-yo] (plan to rest) works too.

49
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:55,589
Before a trip you prepare: '숙소를 예약할 계획이에요' [suk-so-reul-ye-yak-hal-gye-hoek-i-e-yo] — I plan to book a place to stay.

50
00:03:55,589 --> 00:03:58,290
Put today's words into your plans.

51
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:01,301
You can ask, too: '언제 갈 계획이에요?

52
00:04:01,301 --> 00:04:03,606
' — When do you plan to go?

53
00:04:03,606 --> 00:04:06,680
That's how you hear someone's plans.

54
00:04:07,130 --> 00:04:12,217
A real conversation. If asked '제주도에 가 본 적이 있어요?

55
00:04:12,217 --> 00:04:14,922
' (Have you been to Jeju?

56
00:04:14,922 --> 00:04:18,710
), answer '네, 두 번 가 본 적이 있어요' (Yes,

57
00:04:18,710 --> 00:04:20,550
I've been twice).

58
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:25,792
Follow experience with a plan: '두 번 가 본 적이 있어요.

59
00:04:25,792 --> 00:04:27,423
다음엔 부산에 갈 계획이에요.

60
00:04:27,423 --> 00:04:30,890
' — Past and future in one breath!

61
00:04:32,080 --> 00:04:33,173
Your turn.

62
00:04:33,173 --> 00:04:40,500
Think of one place you've been and one you'll go: '___에 가 본 적이 있어요.

63
00:04:40,500 --> 00:04:42,140
___에 갈 계획이에요. '

64
00:04:46,930 --> 00:04:54,998
Recap. Words: 여행·경험·계획·명소·예약·숙소.

65
00:04:54,998 --> 00:05:05,840
Experience: '가 본 적이 있어요'. Plans: '할 계획이에요' [hal-gye-hoek-i-e-yo].

66
00:05:08,690 --> 00:05:09,356
Great job!

67
00:05:09,356 --> 00:05:12,821
Make your own travel story with today's expressions.

68
00:05:12,821 --> 00:05:14,020
See you next time!

